course: french
lesson: 21
title: My house and my home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In French every room of your home has a little word in front of it, le or la, and you learn that word with the room the way you learn a friend's name with their face: la cuisine, la chambre, le salon, le jardin.
explain: The word's gender has nothing to do with you: a boy says ma chambre and a girl says ma chambre too, because chambre is a feminine word.
explain: Picture showing a French penpal round your house: "Voici la cuisine, à côté il y a le salon, et ma chambre est au premier étage" - here is the kitchen, next to it the living room, and my bedroom is one floor up.
explain: Floors are counted as in Britain: the floor at street level is le rez-de-chaussée and le premier étage is one floor up, so an American's "first floor" is a French ground floor.
explain: Small words do big jobs: chez moi means "at my house", à côté de means "next to", and you say à before a town but en before most countries, like J'habite à Cardiff, au pays de Galles.
fact: J'habite dans une maison means "I live in a house"; maison is feminine (une maison) and appartement is masculine (un appartement, a flat).
fact: Le rez-de-chaussée is the ground floor and le premier étage is the first floor up, as in the UK; in the US the "first floor" is the ground floor.
fact: La chambre is the bedroom, la salle de bains is the bathroom, and a room in general is une pièce.
fact: La cuisine means the kitchen and also the cooking, which is why English says "French cuisine".
fact: Place words: sur (on), sous (under), dans (in), devant (in front of), derrière (behind), à côté de (next to), en face de (opposite).
fact: You say à before a town (à Inverness), en before most feminine countries (en Écosse, en France), au before masculine countries (au Canada, au pays de Galles) and aux before plural ones (aux États-Unis).
fact: Un jardin (a garden) is masculine and sounds like "zhar-DAN": a soft zh at the start and a nasal ending with no clear n.
fact: Le grenier is the attic; the word comes from grain, which was once stored up there.
fact: La cave is the cellar, not a cave; a cave in a hill is une grotte.
fact: Chez means "at the home of": chez moi (at my house), chez toi (at yours), chez mamie (at granny's).
fact: Le salon is the living room (masculine) and la salle à manger is the dining room (feminine, because salle is feminine).
fact: You say ma chambre, not mon chambre, because mon and ma follow the gender of the word, not of the person speaking.
exercise: How do you say "I live in a house" in French? | J'habite dans une maison | J'habite dans un maison | Je habite dans une maison | J'habite dans la maisons
exercise: Which word for a home is masculine? | appartement, so it takes un | maison, so it takes un | chambre, so it takes un | cuisine, so it takes un
exercise: Olivia from Chicago says her bedroom is on "le premier étage" but means street level. Where is le premier étage really? | One floor up from street level | At street level, by the front door | Under the house, in the cellar | Right at the top, under the roof
exercise: What is le rez-de-chaussée? | The ground floor | The top floor | The car park | The front garden
exercise: Ruby in Cork asks for "la chambre de bain". What should she have asked for? | la salle de bains | la chambre à bains | la cuisine de bain | le salon de bains
exercise: What is the general French word for any room in a house? | une pièce | une chambre | une salle de bains | un grenier
exercise: Why does English say "French cuisine"? | Because cuisine means cooking as well as kitchen | Because cuisine is the French word for a restaurant | Because cuisine means a fancy dinner party | Because cuisine is the French word for a cook
exercise: Mia says the toilet is "à côté de la cuisine". Where is it? | Next to the kitchen | Inside the kitchen | Opposite the kitchen | Under the kitchen
exercise: Which pair means "on" and "under"? | sur and sous | sur and dans | dans and devant | devant and derrière
exercise: Isla lives in Inverness, in Scotland. Which sentence is right? | J'habite à Inverness, en Écosse | J'habite en Inverness, à Écosse | J'habite à Inverness, à Écosse | J'habite au Inverness, en Écosse
exercise: How does un jardin sound? | Like "zhar-DAN", with a nasal end | Like "jar-din", every letter said | Like "yar-DEEN", with a clear n | Like "shar-DINE", rhyming with mine
exercise: Daniel is shown le grenier. Where is he? | In the attic, up under the roof | In the cellar, down below the house | In the garden shed by the back fence | In the barn, where the grain is kept now
exercise: Your host says the juice is "dans la cave". Where do you go? | Down to the cellar | Out to a cave in the hills | Up to the attic | Into the kitchen cupboard
exercise: Lily's penpal writes "chez moi". What does chez moi mean? | At my house | My cheese | My name is | Near me
exercise: Oscar writes "un salle à manger". What is the fix and why? | une salle à manger, because salle is feminine | un salle à mangé, because the verb ends in é | le salle à manger, because rooms are masculine | une sale à manger, because salle has one l
exercise: Ava, a girl, says "J'ai mon chambre". What should she say? | ma chambre, because chambre is feminine | mon chambre, because she owns the room | ma chambre, because she is a girl | mes chambre, because it is her own room
test: Your mate goes, "Houses are boys in French, innit - un maison." Put him right. | It's une maison: maison is feminine | He's right, all buildings take un | It's la maisons, every house is plural | It's un maisone, with an extra e
test: Tyler from Texas says, "Y'all, my room's on the first floor, same as the front door." His Paris friend hears "premier étage". Where will the friend look first? | One floor up, above the front door | On the floor with the front door | Down in the cellar below the house | In the garden, beside the front door
test: A cartoon cat in a French book is "sous la table". Your little brother draws it on top of the table. What's wrong? | sous means under, so the cat goes beneath the table | Nothing, sous means on top of | sous means behind, so it goes at the back | sous means next to, so it goes by the side
test: Callum from Glasgow writes "J'habite à Écosse." His mate says, "Nae bother, à works for everything." Who's right? | Neither: for Scotland it's en Écosse | Callum: à goes with every place name | His mate: à and en are always the same | Neither: it's au Écosse for Scotland
test: Gran, back from a holiday in Brittany, says, "The cave had wine bottles and a washing machine in it, my lover." (She's from Somerset - "my lover" just means "love".) Why is that normal? | Une cave is a house's cellar, not a cave in a hill | French caves in hills are full of washing machines | Gran mixed up la cave and le grenier, the attic | Une cave is a garden shed for wine and laundry
test: ★★★ Max stays at his gran's at weekends and texts "Je suis à chez mamie." What's the problem? | chez already means "at the home of", so à is extra: Je suis chez mamie | chez needs à before it, so the sentence is fine | chez is a person's name, so it needs a capital C | chez can only go with moi, never with mamie
test: ★★★ A French girl lives in un appartement on le deuxième étage. She walks down two flights of stairs. Where is she now? | On the ground floor, le rez-de-chaussée | On the first floor, le premier étage | In the cellar, la cave | In the attic, le grenier
test: Ben in Newcastle draws a house: "le cuisine, la salon, le jardin". He says, "Howay, two out of three's canny, like." (canny = pretty good) How many are actually right? | One: only le jardin is right | Two: le cuisine and le jardin | All three are right as they are | None: all three are wrong
test: What would happen if you asked a French hotel for "une chambre" when you meant the dining room? | They'd think you wanted a bedroom | They'd show you to the dining room | They'd think you wanted a bathroom | They'd send you down to the cellar
test: ★★★ Which sentence has every little word right? | Ma chambre est à côté de la salle de bains | Mon chambre est à côté de la salle de bains | Ma chambre est à côté du salle de bains | Ma chambre est à côté de le salle de bains
test: Lily from Ohio asks, "Chez - is that like cheese, no cap?" (no cap = honestly) What do you tell her? | No: chez means at someone's home | Yes: chez is how French people say cheese | No: chez is a polite word for sir | Yes: chez means kitchen, where cheese is kept
exam: Identify the grammatically correct rendering of the statement "I reside in a house" in the French language. | J'habite dans une maison | J'habite dans un maison | Je habite dans une maison | J'habite dans des maison
exam: It is given that a dwelling comprises a rez-de-chaussée and a premier étage. Ascertain which of the two is situated at street level. | The rez-de-chaussée | The premier étage | Both, being synonymous terms | Neither, both being below ground
exam: Select the preposition required before the name of a feminine country, such as Écosse, pursuant to the rules of French usage. | en | à | au | aux
exam: Determine which possessive form accompanies the noun chambre, irrespective of the sex of the speaker. | ma, the noun being feminine | mon, the speaker being male | mon, the noun being masculine | mes, the noun being singular
